  "verbalization": "Selegiline, a monoamine oxidase B (MAOB) inhibitor, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Early-onset Parkinsonism-intellectual Disability Syndrome through a series of indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Selegiline is linked to this syndrome via the Hemiparkinsonism-hemiatrophy Syndrome, which in turn is associated with other drugs such as Cycrimine, Entacapone, and Istradefylline. These drugs are known to modulate dopamine metabolism and neuroprotection, which are central to the pathophysiology of Parkinson's disease and related neurodegenerative disorders. The shared mechanism of action—targeting dopamine metabolism and potentially reducing oxidative stress—supports a unified pathway where Selegiline may exert its effects through these intermediaries. The consistent scores across the three paths indicate a robust, albeit indirect, relationship, suggesting that Selegiline may contribute to the syndrome through its influence on dopaminergic pathways, even in the absence of direct therapeutic intervention.",
